</subexercises>
<subexercises xml:id="identity-part-h">
<title>Part H</title>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>In § <xref ref="sec-quantity" text="global"/> we looked at two different translations of ‘Mozart wrote exactly two operas’. Use trees to prove that they are equivalent.</p></statement></exercise>
</subexercises>
<subexercises xml:id="pr-IdentityArguments">
<title>Part I</title>
<introduction>
<p>Translate these arguments into QL with identity, and evaluate them for validity with a tree. (Don’t be surprised or discouraged if some of these trees end up very complex.)</p></introduction>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Dudley will threaten anyone who threatens anyone. Therefore, Dudley will threaten himself.</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>The exam is easy. Therefore, every exam Sheila took was easy. (Use Russell’s theory of definite descriptions.)</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Three wise men visited Jesus. Every wise man who visited Jesus gave Jesus a gift. Therefore, Jesus received more than one gift.</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Worf is the only Klingon in Starfleet. Everyone in Starfleet is brave. All brave Klingons are warriors. Therefore, there is at least one brave warrior in Starfleet.</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Worf is the only Klingon in Starfleet. Everyone in Starfleet is brave. All brave Klingons are warriors. Therefore, there is exactly one brave warrior in Starfleet.</p></statement></exercise>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Every person likes every kind of sandwich that is tasty. Jack is a person. Jack likes exactly one kind of sandwich. Therefore, no more than one kind of sandwich is tasty.</p></statement></exercise>
</subexercises>
<conclusion>
<note component="genai">
<title>GenAI prompts for further practice</title>
<p>Here are some potential GenAI prompts you might use to generate further practice problems, in case you find that helpful.</p>
<p>
<ul>
<li>Give me English sentences involving phrases like “only”, “no one else”, “everyone except”, and “someone other than”. Ask me to translate them into QL with identity. Present one sentence at a time and check my answer.</li>
<li>Give me English sentences making claims about quantities, such as “at least two”, “at most two”, “exactly two”, and “exactly three”. Ask me to translate them into QL with identity and explain how the identity claims ensure that the right number of objects are involved.</li>
<li>Give me English sentences containing definite descriptions, such as “the student who passed the exam”, and ask me to translate them into QL using Russell’s theory of definite descriptions. After I answer, check separately whether my translation expresses existence, uniqueness, and the property attributed to the object.</li>
<li>Give me some identity claims together with atomic and negated atomic QL sentences. Ask me to replace each name with the representative of its identity group and determine whether the resulting sentences reveal a contradiction.</li>
<li>Give me lists of atoms, negated atoms, identity claims, and negated identity claims that could occur on a completed open branch. Ask me to construct a model that satisfies them, making sure that names identified on the branch are assigned the same referent.</li>
<li>Give me sets of QL sentences involving identity and ask me whether I can construct a model satisfying them. Present one set at a time. If I think the set is inconsistent, ask me to explain which identity claims and predicate claims create the conflict.</li></ul></p></note></conclusion>
</exercises>
</chapter>
